# GWK cyclecar, 1911-1920s.
#
# The GWK cyclecar, built by Grice, Wood and Keeler. The 1914 B Type at Goodwood
# is the only car available as a reference and is what is modelled. Its
# distinguishing feature is a rear-mounted transverse twin engine driving
# through friction transmission, so the long low body has no ordinary bonnet
# with a radiator grille: the nose carries a brass unit, modelled as the outline
# the photograph shows, with no invented grille face.
#
# **All dimensions are estimates, not specifications.** No wheelbase, track,
# length, width or height is published in any source consulted. The figures used
# are 3.10 m long, 2.40 m wheelbase, 1.60 m high with the hood raised, 1.05 m
# track (inferred) and 1.26 m across the wings; they come from scaling the
# profile against an assumed 26-inch tyre outside diameter. They must not be
# quoted as specifications.
#
# Shape reference: a single true side elevation of the Goodwood car, with the
# fabric hood raised. The model therefore carries the hood raised as
# photographed; a hood-down variant is not modelled. The front face, the rear
# shape across the width, the track and the cockpit width have no reference and
# were inferred.
#
# Colours: these cars were finished to the buyer's order and no British colour
# list exists. The objects carry plain period-appropriate names: green, black,
# maroon and blue. Weights green 3, black 3, maroon 2, blue 2, total 10,
# provisional pending the central rebase. Brass, fabric, tyres and seats keep
# their own materials.
#
# Speed 70 km/h, an estimate for a cyclecar of this period; no published figure
# was found. Comfort 25/220, the floor of the comfort scale: an open cyclecar
# with a fabric hood. Classes 0,1.
#
# Dates are road presence: 1911 intro, retires staggered 1927-1930 on a
# sixteen- to nineteen-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/gwk-cyclecar.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/gwk-cyclecar.dae (135 objects). References:
# citycars/references/gwk-cyclecar/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: only one side photograph exists, so the front face, the
# rear shape and the track are inferred; the canopy's rear quarter and the wing
# profiles are simplified. At 128 px the low body, the narrow track, the open
# seats, the flat wings and the raised fabric hood read as a GWK cyclecar.

obj=citycar
name=GWKCyclecar(Green)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=3
intro_year=1911
intro_month=1
retire_year=1930
retire_month=6
speed=70
class=0,1
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=220

